@*
    A chevron pointing up: two strokes meeting at a peak.

    Drawn here rather than fetched, for the reason DeleteButton's and CopyButton's
    glyphs give: a control that needs the network to show itself is no use in a
    WebView with no egress. There is no sprite file, no icon font and no CDN — the
    shape is in this file, and nothing downstream ships a second asset.

    Lucide's idiom rather than Lucide's file, which is what
    `.design/typography-and-layout.md#iconography` allows on exactly these terms:
    an outline on the 24x24 grid, stroked at 2, round caps and joins, filled
    nowhere. The whole shape is stated in the grid's own units, so a caller
    changing the size changes nothing about the drawing.

    One direction, and no state in here at all. Which state a drawing stands for is
    the host's rule, so this file ships no `Pinned` parameter and no second drawing
    behind one — that decision belongs where two hosts with two conventions can each
    have their own, which is the call site rather than the library. The desktop
    shell's section rail reads the pair of directions as its states and turns the
    glyph in its own stylesheet: up while a pane is loose, so the control says push,
    and half a turn down once pushed, so it says push back. Nothing about that
    reaches in here, and a host that reads a chevron some other way — a disclosure
    that opens downward, a sort that runs ascending — gets the same drawing.

    Hidden from assistive technology by default, because the control that holds a
    glyph is where the name belongs: the shell's rail is a ToggleButton carrying
    its own `aria-label`, and a named glyph inside a named button is the label read
    twice. The attribute is written before the splat, so a caller that has taken
    the word off the screen can still override it.

    Nothing is coloured or sized here beyond what the caller asks for: the chapter
    rules out a hard-coded stroke and a font-size scale alike, so the ink comes from
    whatever the glyph is sitting in and the edge length is an explicit width and
    height.
*@

<svg class="@ClassValue"
     viewBox="0 0 24 24"
     width="@Size"
     height="@Size"
     fill="none"
     stroke="currentColor"
     stroke-width="2"
     stroke-linecap="round"
     stroke-linejoin="round"
     aria-hidden="true"
     focusable="false"
     data-testid="@TestId"
     @attributes="AdditionalAttributes">
    @* One open outline: up from the right foot, over the peak on the grid's
       centre line, and down to the left foot. *@
    <path d="M18 15 12 9 6 15" />
</svg>

@code {
    /// <summary>Edge length, written straight onto <c>width</c> and <c>height</c>.
    /// <para>A bare number is the grid's own pixels, which is how the iconography
    /// scale states its sizes — 12 for the inline indicator, 16 inline with text,
    /// 20 in a button. Sixteen by default, the size the library's other glyphs are
    /// drawn at. A unit is allowed too, for a caller that wants the glyph to scale
    /// with the type it leads.</para></summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string Size { get; set; } = "16";

    [Parameter]
    public string? CssClass { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? TestId { get; set; }

    [Parameter(CaptureUnmatchedValues = true)]
    public IReadOnlyDictionary<string, object>? AdditionalAttributes { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The glyph names itself even though this library styles nothing on
    /// it: a host that has to reach the drawing inside its own control then has a
    /// hook that says what it is holding rather than an element selector that would
    /// catch any glyph put there. A host that moves or turns the drawing for a state
    /// of its own — as the section rail does, which lifts it on hover and turns it
    /// once pinned — passes its own class through <c>CssClass</c> and styles that,
    /// so the rule says which control it is about and not merely which shape.</summary>
    private string ClassValue =>
        string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(CssClass) ? "chevron-up-icon" : $"chevron-up-icon {CssClass}";
}
